  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2s+t=7 \\\\ \\frac{3s}{8}+\\frac{t}{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the system of equations given above.\n\n$s=$\n{{field-1}}$,\\,t=$\n{{field-2}}",
  "worked_solution": "To get rid of the fractions in the second equation, we must multiply the second equation by the lowest common denominator of the fractions, which is $8.$\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2s+t=7 \\\\ \\frac{3s}{8}+\\frac{t}{2}=1 & |\\,\\times 8\\end{aligned}\\,\u27f9\\,{\\begin{aligned}2s+t=7 \\\\ 3s+4t=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe will use the substitution method to solve the system. Let's solve for $t$ in the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2s+t & =7 \\\\ t & =7-2s\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we can substitute $t=7-2s$ into the second equation and solve for $s:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3s+4t & =8 \\\\ 3s+4(7-2s) & =8 \\\\ 3s+28-8s & =8 \\\\ -5s+28 & =8 \\\\ -5s & =-20 \\\\ s & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to find the value of $t,$ we substitute $s=4$ into the equation $t=7-2s,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =7-2s \\\\  & =7-2(4) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the system of equations is $s=4,\\,t=-1.$",
